meta {
  name: rate-dish-invalid
  type: http
  seq: 3
}

put {
  url: {{base_url}}/api/guest/v1/orders/{{order_uid}}/items/{{order_item_uid}}/review
  body: json
  auth: none
}

headers {
  X-Guest-Token: {{guest_token}}
  Content-Type: application/json
}

body:json {
  {
    "rating": 4,
    "tags": ["delicious"]
  }
}

tests {
  test("refuses a tag outside the vocabulary", function () {
    // A DELIBERATE REFUSAL. Verifying that this is rejected is worth more than a green exit
    // code -- see the collection's docs on why the runner reads assertion results rather than
    // Bruno's exit status.
    expect(res.status).to.equal(422)
  })

  test("with a code the client can switch on", function () {
    expect(res.body.code).to.equal("TX_REV_003")
  })

  test("and the earlier rating is untouched", function () {
    // The whole request is refused, so nothing partial was written. A rejected tag must not
    // leave the rating updated -- that would be a validation error that half-succeeded.
    expect(res.body.data).to.be.undefined
  })
}

docs {
  # A tag outside the vocabulary

`tags` is a closed set, and an unrecognised value is a 422 rather than a silent drop.

Silently discarding it would let a client ship a typo that looks like it works, and produce a tag
count nobody can explain later. The vocabulary lives in `backend/internal/models/review.go` and is
mirrored in `packages/shared/src/review.ts`.
}
